{
  "abstract": "A Lean 4 formalisation of the solvability theory of linear second-order elliptic operators in divergence form on a bounded domain,\n\n  -div(A grad u) + b · grad u + c u = f in Ω,   u = 0 on ∂Ω,\n\nwith A uniformly elliptic and every coefficient bounded and measurable. The drift b is unrestricted, so the bilinear form is in general non-symmetric and the problem has no variational structure; existence runs through Lax-Milgram.\nProved with no `sorry` in the library: the Gårding inequality for the general operator on any domain; existence and uniqueness of the weak solution, which needs a coercive form and so is proved for a bounded domain with no drift and a nonnegative zeroth-order coefficient; the Fredholm alternative for the general operator on a bounded domain, which is what governs solvability when the form is not coercive, together with its solvability criterion against the transpose problem; the discrete set of μ off which L u = μ u + f is uniquely solvable, and boundedness of the inverse off that set; spectral compactness of the operator; interior H² regularity; higher interior regularity at every order; infinite differentiability in the interior; and interior Hölder continuity at exponent 1/2 in dimensions one, two and three.\nSupporting layers supply Morrey's inequality, the Gagliardo-Nirenberg-Sobolev estimate in general dimension and at a general exponent pair, Campanato's characterisation of Hölder continuity with its converse, the Caccioppoli inequality, and the Poincaré chain down to the one-dimensional inequality. H_0^1(Ω) is realised as the closure of the test-function graphs inside L²(Ω) × (L²(Ω))^d, so a member has its weak gradient as data.\nThe Hilbert-space theory is also stated over a finite-dimensional real inner product space E with no coordinates: the graph space of pairs (u, ∇u) in L²(Ω) × L²(Ω; E), a coefficient field a : E → (E →L E), and a weak derivative built on Mathlib's distributions. The Gårding inequality, Lax-Milgram existence, Poincaré on a bounded set, Rellich-Kondrachov, the Fredholm alternative, the exceptional set, the resolvent bound, the symmetric spectral theorem, the weak maximum principles, interior smoothness and the H^{-1} characterisation are proved there, the bounded-domain results for any additive Haar measure; the Fredholm and spectral proofs are written once for an abstract shifted-coercive form on a Hilbert space. The coordinate statements, the six compared here among them, are the instances at E = R^d.\nThree further layers sit above that base. The maximum principles: the weak principle for the divergence-form operator with and without a transport term, the classical weak and strong principles for the non-divergence operator, Hopf's lemma, the comparison and avoidance principles, uniqueness for the classical Dirichlet and Neumann problems, and the a priori bound. The W^{1,p} extension operator across a C¹ boundary, with the global approximation by functions smooth up to the boundary it supplies, and with Rellich-Kondrachov on H^1(Ω) and the Poincaré-Wirtinger inequality above it. The variational spectral theory: the direct method under a subcritical constraint, the Dirichlet eigenvalue sequence by Rayleigh quotients, and the sharp Poincaré constant.\nBoundary H² regularity has its foundations in place and its headline estimate open, and Schauder C^{k,α} estimates remain a roadmap item.\nThe Comparator configuration of this submission selects six declarations and no others, the solvability theory of Evans §6.2: the Gårding inequality, existence and uniqueness for a bounded domain with no drift and a nonnegative zeroth-order coefficient, the Fredholm alternative, solvability against the transpose problem, the discrete set of exceptional μ, and boundedness of the inverse. It excludes the interior-regularity results of the library, which are interior H² regularity, higher interior regularity, infinite differentiability in the interior and interior Hölder continuity, along with the spectral results and the Poincaré chain. Those are listed under status.main_results without a Comparator configuration and will take their own challenge and solution modules in a later submission.",
